##Git revert:

git revert creates a new commit that undoes an earlier commit.

It is the safer option when the commit has already been pushed to a shared branch.

Example:

git log --oneline

Suppose:

abc1234 Add login
def5678 Add payment

You want to undo def5678:

git revert def5678

Git creates a new commit:

abc1234 Add login
def5678 Add payment
xyz9999 Revert "Add payment"
Important

The original commit is still in history. Git adds another commit to reverse its changes.

Use it for:

main
develop
shared branches
commits already pushed to remote
#### 2. git reset

git reset moves the branch's HEAD backward.

Suppose:

A → B → C
        ↑
       HEAD

Run:

git reset --hard B

Now:

A → B
    ↑
   HEAD

Commit C is no longer part of the current branch history.

There are three important reset modes.

--soft
git reset --soft HEAD~1

Removes the commit but keeps the changes staged.

Commit removed
     ↓
Changes → Staged

Useful when you want to modify/recreate the commit.

--mixed — default
git reset HEAD~1

Removes the commit and keeps the changes in your working directory, but unstaged.

Commit removed
     ↓
Changes → Unstaged
--hard
git reset --hard HEAD~1

Removes the commit and removes the changes from the working directory.
